into
\[ \hand{$\Delta$}_2 = \hand{$a\longrightarrow b\longrightarrow c$} \]
be cofibering. Namely, for a category C, to say that it is``fibering''
(over the final category e) with respect to one or the other monomorphism,
means respectively that every arrow in $\underline{C}$ has a left respectively a right
inverse - the two together mean that $\underline{C}$ is a groupoid. But groupoids are
definitely \uline{not} sufficient for describing arbitrary homotopy types, they
give rise only to sums of K($\pi$,1) spaces \xout{\unsure{(}} - thus contradicting Quillen's
statement that homotopy types can be described by ``models'' which are b\unsure{o}th
fibering and cofibering\hand{!}

The feeling however remains that any elementary modelizer, namely one
defined (up to equivalence) by a test category, should be a \inserted{closed} model catego\unsure{ry}
% NOTE: the end of this line is cut by the right edge of the scan
in Quillen's sense - \over{\unsure{If}}{I} find it hard to believe that this should be a spe%
% NOTE: the end of this line (hyphen?) is cut by the right edge of the scan
cial feature just of semi-simplicial complexes !

While trying to understand test categories, the notion of \uline{asphericity}
for a morphism of topoi
\[ f\colon X \hand{$\longrightarrow$} Y \]
came in naturally - this is a natural \xout{\illegible{1}} variant of the notion of
n-acyclicity (concerned with commutative cohomology) which has been deve-%
lopped \unsure{in|fn} the context of étale topology of schemes in SGA 4. It can be
expressed by ``localizing\xout{\illegible{1}} upon Y '' the Artin-Mazur condition that f be
a weak equivalence, by demanding that the same remain true for the induced
morphisms of topoi
\[ X\mathrm{x}_{Y}Y' \hand{$\longrightarrow$} Y' \]
for any ``localization morphism'' Y'\hand{$\longrightarrow$} Y. In terms of the categories of
sheaves \xout{\illegible{1}} E, F on X,Y , Y' can be defined by an object (equally called
Y') of F, the category of sheaves on Y' being F$_{/Y'}$ , and the fiber-pro-%
duct X' can \unsure{be} defined likewise \unsure{by} an object \deleted{\unsure{X'}} of E, namely \inserted{by (X'=} f$^{*}$(Y'),
hence the corresponding category of sheaves is E$_{/f^{*}(Y')}$ . In case the
functor f$^{*}$ associated to f admits a left adjoint f$_{\hand{!}}$ (namely if \unsure{it|ft} commutes
to arbitrary inverse limits, not only to finite one's), the category
E$_{/f^{*}(Y')}$ can be interpreted conveniently as \xout{E$_{/Y'}$} E$_{f_{\hand{!}}/Y'}$ (or simply E$_{/Y'}$
if f$_{\hand{!}}$ is implicit), whose objects are pairs
\[ (U,\emptyset) \quad , \quad U \hand{$\in$} \mathrm{Ob}\ E \ , \ \emptyset : f_{\hand{!}}(U) \hand{$\longrightarrow$} Y' \quad , \]
with obvious ``maps'' between such objects. For the time being I am mainly
interested in the case of a morphism of topoi defined by a functor \over{\unsure{(}}{b}etween
categories, which I will denote by the same symbol \hand{$\hat{\ }$}f :
\[ f\colon C' \hand{$\longrightarrow$} C \quad \text{defines f or } \hand{$\hat{\ }$}f : \hand{$\hat{\ }$}C\hand{$'$} \hand{$\to$} \hand{$\hat{\ }$}C\xout{\unsure{'}} \quad . \]

