40. Yesterday I incidentally made use of the fact that if A is an asphe-%
ric element in (Cat), th\over{\unsure{a}}{e}n for any other object C, CxA \hand{$\longrightarrow$} C is aspheric
(and a fortiori a weak equivalence). The usual asphericity criterion for a
functor shows that it is enough to prove that for any C with final element,
\margin{$\downarrow$}CxA is aspheric. \xout{Thusxw} For this again it is enough to prove that the
projection CxA \hand{$\longrightarrow$} A is aspheric, which by localisation up\over{\unsure{n}}{o}n A means that
the product categories \hand{.} Cx\hand{(}$A_{/a}$\hand{)} (with a in A) are aspheric. Finall\over{\unsure{x}}{y} we
are reduced to checking that the product of two categories with final ele-%
ment is aspheric, which is trivial because such a category has itself a
final element.

The result just proved can be viewed as a particular case of the
following
\uline{Proposition} In (Cat), the cartesian product of two weak equivalences is
a weak equivalence.

