of an aspheric interval \xout{which} \uline{I} which is contractible  -  namely when
\uline{I} is even \uline{I}-contractible,  and more specifically still, \xout{xx} \inserted[typed]{when} there exists
a\xout{nhomotopy} \uline{I}-homotopy (an ``elementary'',  not a ``composed'' one !) \xout{brxn}
from id$_I$ to one of the two constant maps of I into itself,  defined by the
two sections $\hand{$\delta$}_0$,$\hand{$\delta$}_1$  - say by $\hand{$\delta$}_1$ , to be specific.  Such a homotopy is a
map
\[ h\colon IxI \to I \]
in M,  having the two properties expressed symbolically by
\[ h(e_0,x) = x \quad , \quad h(e_1,x) = e_1 \quad , \]
where x may be viewed as any ``point'' of I with ``values'' in an arbitrary
parameter object T of M,  i.e.  $x : T \to I$ ,  and $e_0$ , $e_1$ are the constant
maps $T \to I$ defined by $\hand{$\delta$}_0,\hand{$\delta$}_1$.  If we view h as a composition law on I,
these relations just mean that $e_0$ acts as a left unit,  and $e_1$ as a left
zero element.  Such composition law in an interval I,  a would-be homotopy
interval as a matter of fact,  has been repetitively used before,  and
this use systematized in the ``comparison lemma for homotopy intervals''
(page 60);  \over{T}{t}here we found that if I is an object in M with such a composi-%
tion law,  and if \xout{Jxix} \inserted[typed]{there exists} an \uline{aspheric} interval \uline{J} and a morphism of intervals
from \uline{J} into \uline{I} ,  then I is equally aspheric. \unsure{\over{t}{T}|\hand{T}}his we would now see as
coming from the fact that I being \uline{I}-contractible is  \uline{J}-contractible, and
a fortiori (as \uline{J} is aspheric over e) aspheric over e. \hand{In any case,}
\hand{we see that} when I is endowed with a composition law as above, then
I is aspheric over e iff it is contractible,  and equivalently iff \hand{$\delta_0$} and
\hand{$\delta_1$} are \inserted{W-}homotopic. \margin{20}

