with obvious maps \over{$\phi_o^+$}{$\psi_o^+$} , \over{$\phi_o^-$}{$\psi_o^-$} : $D_o \to S_o$ , and then \hand{,}if everything is constru\hand{-}
ted up to $D_n$ \deleted{\unsure{,}} and $S_n = (D_n,\phi_{n-1}^+)\amalg_{D_{n-1}}(D_n,\phi_{n-1}^-)$\hand{,} defining $D_{n+1}$ \deleted{\illegible{1}}\inserted{as} any
\margin{$D_{n+1}$}fibrant and trivial\inserted{(16)} object together with a cofibrant map
\[ S_n \longrightarrow D_{n+1} , \]
and $\phi_n^+ , \phi_n^-$ as the compositions of the latter with \hand{$\psi_n^+,\psi_n^-$} : $D_n \rightrightarrows S_n$ .
Using this and amalgamated sums in M, we get our functor
\[ \hand{$\underline{B}_o =$}\, \underline{C}_o^{\,o} \longrightarrow M \quad , \quad K \longmapsto |K|_M , \]
commuting with amalgamated sums, which we can extend stepwise through
