There is another amusing interpretation of the homotopy type associa-%
ted to any preordered set K,  via the topological space whose underlying
\margin{$\times$} set is K itself,  and where the closed sets are the subsets J of K
such that  $x \hand{\in} J$,  $y \hand{\unsure{\leq|\subseteq}} x$ implies $y \hand{\in} J$.  This is a highly non-separated
topology\inserted{$\mathcal{T}$} (except when the preorder relation is the discrete one),  where
an arbitrary union of closed subsets is again closed.  I doubt it's
singular homotopy type to make much sense,  however it's  homotopy type
as a topos does,  and (possibly under mild loc\unsure{\hand{-}}al finiteness restriction\unsure{s}\hand{)}
it should be the same as the homotopy type of K just envisioned.  Thus,
a sheaf on the topological space K  \xout{defines} can be interpreted \xout{as being}
\unsure{V}ia its fibers as being just a \uline{covariant} functor
