a) stability \xout{with}\inserted{by} compositions $D_n \to |K| \to |K'|$, where $K \to K'$
is \xout{a}\inserted{an ``allowable''} continuous map, \inserted{namely} subjected only to the condition that its restriction
to any standar\over{t}{d} subcell $D_{n'} \subset |K|$ is again ``allowable'' i.e. in $\mathrm{Hom}_\infty$ .
b) Any ``allowable'' map $S_n^{\unsure{*}} \to |K|$ (i.e. whose restrictions to $S_n^+$ and
$S_n^-$ are allowable) extends to an allowable map $D_{n+1} \to |K|$.

Condition a) merely ensures stability of allowable maps under compo-%
sition, and the fact that $\underline{B}_\infty$ (endowed with the allowable maps as morphi\hand{s}-%
-ms) has the correct binary amalgamated sums, whereas b) expresses condi-%
tion \unsure{A|a}) on $\underline{C}_\infty$ . These conditions are satisfied when we take as $\mathrm{Hom}_\infty$
subsets defined by tameness conditions (such as piecewise linear for
suitable piecewise linear structure\inserted{on the $D_i$'s}, or differentiable etc). The condi-%
tion (B) however is of a subtler nature in \unsure{this|the} topological \xout{a}interpretation\hand{,}
and surely not met by such sweeping tameness requirements only ! Finally
the question as to whether we can actually in this way describe an ``accep-%
table'' category $\underline{C}_\infty$ , by defining sets $\mathrm{Hom}_\infty$ namely describing $\underline{C}_\infty$ in terms
of $\underline{B}_\infty$ , seems rather subsidiary\inserted{after all}. We may think of course of constructing
stepwise $\underline{B}_\infty$ via subcategories $\underline{B}_n$ , by adding stepwise new arrows in or-%
der to meet condition b), thus paraphrasing condition (B) for passage \xout{of}
from $\underline{C}_n$ to $\underline{C}_{n+1}$ - but it is by no means clear that when passing to the
\xout{sub}category $\underline{B}_{n+1}$ by composing \xout{arrows} maps of $\underline{B}_n$ and ``new'' ones, and
using amalgamated sums too, there might not be some \inserted{undesirable} extra relations
\xout{arising} in $\underline{B}_{n+1}$ , coming from the topological interpretation of the arrows
in $\underline{C}_{n+1}$ as maps. To say it differently, universal algebra furnishes us
readily with an acceptable sequence of categories $\underline{C}_n$ and hence $\underline{C}_\infty$ , and
by the universal properties of the $\underline{C}_n$ in terms of $\underline{C}_{n-1}$ , we readily get
(using arbitrary choices) a contravariant functor $K \mapsto |K|$ from $\underline{C}_\infty$ to
the category of topological spaces (i.e. a co-$\infty$-groupoid in (Top)), but
it is by no means clear that this functor is \uline{faithful} - and it does'nt
really matter after all !

